\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Wilson and Villain coefficients on the circle}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For #{G=U(1)}, use characters #{\chi_n(\ue^{\ui\theta})=\ue^{\ui n\theta}}
with #{n\in\mathbb Z} and normalized Haar measure
#{\frac{\extd\theta}{2\pi}}. For #{\beta>0}, the Wilson weight
#{w_\beta(\theta)=\ue^{\beta\cos\theta}} has coefficients
##{I_n(\beta)=\sum_{j=0}^\infty
 \frac{(\beta /2)^{2j+|n|}}{j!(j+|n|)!}>0,
 \qquad \sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}I_n(\beta)=\ue^\beta.}
Thus its character series is uniformly absolutely convergent.
The \vocabk{Wilson action convention}{fdip-006O} includes the additional
constant factor #{\ue^{-\beta}}; this multiplies all coefficients
but cancels from the normalized one-plaquette probability.}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{Real.integral_exp_mul_cos_mul_cexp}
proves the Wilson circle integral with exponent #{+\ui n\theta} and
unnormalized measure on #{[-\pi,\pi]}. Replacing #{n} by #{-n}
and dividing by #{2\pi} gives the coefficient convention here;
its Bessel value has the displayed power series. This correspondence
supports the Wilson coefficient identity, not the Villain formula
or the comparison of the two weights.}

\p{For #{\beta_V>0}, the Villain weight is the periodic Gaussian
##{v_{\beta_V}(\theta)=\sum_{\ell\in\mathbb Z}
 \exp\left[-\frac{\beta_V}{2}(\theta-2\pi\ell)^2\right].}
Its character coefficient is
##{c_n^V=(2\pi\beta_V)^{-1/2}
 \ue^{-n^2/(2\beta_V)}.}
The coefficient series is again uniformly absolutely convergent and
all coefficients are strictly positive. Dividing each weight by its
zeroth coefficient normalizes its integral to one, giving
##{\widehat c_n^W=\frac{I_n(\beta)}{I_0(\beta)},\qquad
 \widehat c_n^V=\ue^{-n^2/(2\beta_V)}.}
These normalized coefficients differ from the unnormalized
#{c_\rho} in the [surface formula](fdip-007C).}

\proof{
\p{Put #{z=\ue^{\ui\theta}}. Multiply the two absolutely convergent
series for #{\exp(\beta z/2)} and #{\exp(\beta z^{-1}/2)}.
The coefficient of #{z^n} is the displayed #{I_n}; the total of
all absolute coefficients is the product #{\ue^{\beta /2}\ue^{\beta /2}}.
Every #{I_n} is positive for #{\beta>0}.}

\p{For the periodic Gaussian, uniform Gaussian tail bounds on a
period justify continuity and integration term by term. Unfolding
its coefficient integral gives
##{c_n^V=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{\mathbb R}
 \ue^{-\beta_V x^2/2}\ue^{-\ui nx}\,\extd x.}
For real #{t}, let #{J(t)} be the integral without #{1/(2\pi)}
and with #{n} replaced by #{t}. Gaussian domination permits
differentiation under the integral. Integration by parts, with
vanishing boundary terms, gives
#{J'(t)=-(t/\beta_V)J(t)}. Since
#{J(0)=\sqrt{2\pi /\beta_V}}, this proves the coefficient formula.
The Gaussian coefficient bound is summable over #{n}. Its uniformly
convergent Fourier series has the same coefficients as the continuous
periodic Gaussian, so completeness of the [character basis](fdip-007C)
and continuity identify the two functions. Finally, the integral of a
weight is its zeroth coefficient, which proves the normalization.}
}

\p{Both positive-parameter weights satisfy the
[positive-character kernel hypotheses](fdip-007I): they are real,
strictly positive, invariant under inversion, and have positive
summable coefficients. For #{U(1)} every irreducible dimension is
one. This checks the kernel assumptions without asserting any
transfer-operator or continuum conclusion.}
